    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03389-z",
    "abstract": "Abstract Urban green spaces provide vital benefits to human well-being, yet increasingly frequent summer heat and humidity undermine their use and value. Here we quantify welfare losses attributable to reduced urban green space use under intensifying summer heat and humidity. We find that high temperature and humidity substantially reduce annual recreational value, with losses over 22 million United States dollars under the most extreme conditions. However, features such as water play areas and air-conditioned indoor facilities partially offset these losses. Our results highlight the vulnerability of urban recreation to climate extremes and demonstrate how targeted cooling infrastructure can support continued use of green spaces. These findings provide evidence to inform climate adaptation strategies aimed at protecting public well-being in cities experiencing rapidly warming summers.",
    "field": "Environmental economics",
    "problem": "Existing research recognizes that extreme heat and humidity reduce urban green space use, but lacks a monetary valuation of the resulting welfare loss, which is crucial for developing effective climate adaptation strategies to protect public well-being in cities.",
    "method": "This study proposes an integrated approach, combining a travel cost model with mobile phone GPS data and a discrete choice experiment, to monetize the welfare loss from reduced urban green space use under rising temperatures and humidity.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the monetary welfare losses from the reduced recreational value and use of urban green spaces caused by extreme summer heat and humidity.",
